LIVEBENCH — encodebench's work, against the library that ships.

This directory imports `std/json`. Its sibling bench/encodebench vendors a
snapshot of lib/json frozen at 20ab931d (2026-08-07) and must keep doing so:
with the library held still, a move in that benchmark is attributable to the
COMPILER alone, and kanso#1230 is the recorded case where that control did the
separating.

The cost of the freeze is that nothing then watches the LIBRARY's encode path
at load. bench/jsonbench is generated from lib/json but only decodes;
bench/widebench carries a second frozen copy; bench/oneshot imports the real
library and encodes exactly once. So a change to the library's encoder was
measured, in practice, by a single encode of a 185 KB document inside a
benchmark whose bulk is the decode beside it.

The two directories run the same program on the same input for the same number
of rounds, and both print the same checksum, 74072800. Reading them side by
side is the point: their difference is the library's drift away from the frozen
snapshot, and it was 10,149,724 instructions — 0.19% — on the day this was
written. A frozen control and a live twin answer different questions, and the
corpus had only the first.
